            <ArticleTitle>Acute ST-elevation inferior myocardial infarction in a patient with a non-obstructive mechanical mitral valve thrombosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e7-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5830/CVJA-2012-047</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The risk of systemic embolisation in patients with prosthetic heart valves who are receiving anticoagulation therapy is 0.5 to 1.7% per patient year and most cases present with cerebrovascular events. Here we report the case of a 42-year-old woman who was uneventfully treated with a low dose, prolonged infusion of tissue plasminogen activator because of non-obstructive prosthetic mitral valve thrombosis. It presented as coronary embolism and resulted in acute ST-elevation inferior myocardial infarction.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
